 "asr_text": "She'd never stolen anything, not even a paper clip. Then she read one sign and wanted to steal. Arizona's Petrified Forest. Visitors kept pocketing little pieces, so the park put up a warning. said, 14 tons stolen every year, one small piece at a time. She read it, leaned over to her fiance, and whispered, we'd better get ours now. But why would a warning make an honest person want to steal. Her fiancee's professor, psychologist Robert Cialdini, tested it. His team hid marked wood along the trails and kept swapping the signs. One sign said, please don't take the wood. The other said, many visitors take the wood. With please don't, only 2 % of the pieces vanished. With many visitors, almost 8 %! You see, we don't copy rules. We copy each other. Many people steal doesn't sound like a warning it sounds like what everyone does like a teacher saying Half this class cheated the other half hears Everybody cheats the sign didn't stop thieves it gave them permission and the bait would It came from the parks conscience pile pieces people stole then mailed back with apology letters So some of it got stolen twice",
